---
// Card body for /notes/isotropic-gradient-hessian. Rendered on its own page and inside every series that uses it.
import Tex from '../components/Tex.astro';
---
  <h2 id="setup">Setup</h2>
  <p>
    As in <a href="/notes/three-ways-to-write-a-material">Three Ways to Write an Isotropic Material</a>, an isotropic energy density depends on the
    deformation gradient only through its principal stretches: with the signed SVD
    <Tex tex={String.raw`F=U\,\mathrm{diag}(\lambda_1,\lambda_2,\lambda_3)\,V^{\mathsf T}`} />,
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\Psi(F)=\psi(\lambda_1,\lambda_2,\lambda_3)`} />. A Newton solver needs two things from it: the
    first Piola–Kirchhoff stress <Tex tex={String.raw`P=\partial\Psi/\partial F`} /> for the residual, and the Hessian
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\partial^2\Psi/\partial F^2`} /> (9×9 in 3D) for the tangent stiffness. Write
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\psi_i=\partial\psi/\partial\lambda_i`} /> and
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\psi_{ij}=\partial^2\psi/\partial\lambda_i\partial\lambda_j`} />, and
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf u_i,\mathbf v_i`} /> for the columns of <Tex tex="U, V" />.
  </p>
  <p>
    Stretch-based materials had a reputation problem: the formulas for these derivatives divide by differences of stretches,
    and naive implementations blow up when two stretches become equal, which includes the undeformed state. In our SIGGRAPH
    Asia 2023 paper we needed stretch-based materials to be robust, because we were fitting them to measurements inside an
    optimization loop, so we worked out the complete picture. Here it is in short.
  </p>

  <h2 id="gradient">The gradient: diagonal in the SVD frame</h2>
  <Tex block tex={String.raw`P=\frac{\partial\Psi}{\partial F}=U\,\mathrm{diag}(\psi_1,\psi_2,\psi_3)\,V^{\mathsf T}=\sum_i\psi_i\,\mathbf u_i\mathbf v_i^{\mathsf T}.`} />
  <p>
    The quick argument: a small change <Tex tex={String.raw`dF`} /> moves each stretch by
    <Tex tex={String.raw`d\lambda_i=\mathbf u_i^{\mathsf T}dF\,\mathbf v_i`} />, and the energy ignores the change of the
    rotations. The careful argument (Appendix C of the paper) chains through the lower invariants
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\operatorname{tr}S,\ \|F\|^2,\ \det F`} />, whose derivatives with respect to <Tex tex="F" /> have no
    singularities. It also settles a question the quick argument skips: when two stretches are equal, <Tex tex="U" /> and
    <Tex tex="V" /> are not unique (any rotation within the repeated plane works). Because <Tex tex={String.raw`\psi_1=\psi_2`} />
    whenever <Tex tex={String.raw`\lambda_1=\lambda_2`} />, the formula only depends on
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf u_1\mathbf v_1^{\mathsf T}+\mathbf u_2\mathbf v_2^{\mathsf T}`} />, which is the same for every
    valid choice. The stress is well defined, as it must be.
  </p>

  <h2 id="hessian">The Hessian: one 3×3 block and six numbers</h2>
  <p>
    Unroll 3×3 matrices into 9-vectors and build an orthonormal basis of <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbb R^9`} /> from the SVD
    (Appendix D; the same basis as Smith, de Goes and Kim, 2019):
  </p>
  <Tex block tex={String.raw`\mathbf d_i=\mathrm{vec}(\mathbf u_i\mathbf v_i^{\mathsf T}),\qquad
    \mathbf t_{k}=\tfrac{1}{\sqrt2}\mathrm{vec}(\mathbf u_i\mathbf v_j^{\mathsf T}-\mathbf u_j\mathbf v_i^{\mathsf T}),\qquad
    \mathbf l_{k}=\tfrac{1}{\sqrt2}\mathrm{vec}(\mathbf u_i\mathbf v_j^{\mathsf T}+\mathbf u_j\mathbf v_i^{\mathsf T}),`} />
  <p>
    one <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf t_k`} /> and one <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf l_k`} /> for each pair
    <Tex tex="(i, j)" />. The <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf d_i`} /> change the stretches ("scaling"), the
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf t_k`} /> rotate within the <Tex tex="ij" /> plane ("twist") and the
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\mathbf l_k`} /> shear it ("flip"). In this basis the Hessian is block-diagonal (the paper's Figure 6):
  </p>
  <Tex block tex={String.raw`\frac{\partial^2\Psi}{\partial F^2}=B\,\mathrm{diag}\!\Big(\big[\psi_{ij}\big]_{3\times3},\ \
    \frac{\psi_i+\psi_j}{\lambda_i+\lambda_j}\ \text{(twist)},\ \
    \frac{\psi_i-\psi_j}{\lambda_i-\lambda_j}\ \text{(flip)}\Big)\,B^{\mathsf T},\qquad B=(\mathbf d_1,\mathbf d_2,\mathbf d_3,\mathbf t_1,\mathbf t_2,\mathbf t_3,\mathbf l_1,\mathbf l_2,\mathbf l_3).`} />
  <p>
    So the nine eigenvalues are the three eigenvalues of the 3×3 matrix <Tex tex={String.raw`[\psi_{ij}]`} /> plus six
    quotients, and the eigenvectors are known in closed form. The block-diagonal structure itself is old (it goes back to
    J. M. Ball's 1984 work on symmetric and isotropic functions, and appears for invariant-based energies in Teran et al.,
    2005); what the paper contributes for stretch-based materials is a complete, explicit statement with this basis, proofs,
    the handling of the singular cases below, and the projection to positive definiteness.
  </p>
  <p>
    Where do the quotients come from? In 2D, perturb <Tex tex={String.raw`\Sigma`} /> by
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\varepsilon\begin{bmatrix}0&a\\b&0\end{bmatrix}`} />. The stretches do not move at first order;
    at second order, the energy changes by
  </p>
  <Tex block tex={String.raw`\frac{\varepsilon^2}{2}\left[\frac{\psi_1}{\lambda_1}\Big(b^2+\frac{c^2}{\lambda_1^2-\lambda_2^2}\Big)+\frac{\psi_2}{\lambda_2}\Big(a^2-\frac{c^2}{\lambda_1^2-\lambda_2^2}\Big)\right],
    \qquad c=\lambda_1a+\lambda_2b.`} />
  <p>
    Setting <Tex tex="a = -b" /> (twist) or <Tex tex="a = b" /> (flip) and dividing by <Tex tex={String.raw`a^2+b^2`} />
    gives the two quotients, and the cross term between them vanishes. I checked all nine 3D eigenvalues numerically
    against a finite-difference Hessian; the demo repeats the 2D check live.
  </p>

  <h2 id="singular">0/0 when stretches meet, and why it is harmless</h2>
  <p>
    The flip quotient <Tex tex={String.raw`(\psi_i-\psi_j)/(\lambda_i-\lambda_j)`} /> is 0/0 when
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\lambda_i=\lambda_j`} />: at the rest shape, under uniform scaling, and in every symmetric
    deformation. By symmetry the numerator vanishes there too, and Appendix D.1 of the paper shows the singularity is
    removable, with limit <Tex tex={String.raw`\psi_{11}-\psi_{12}`} />. How to evaluate the quotient accurately near that
    point, and how three of my papers do it, is the subject of
    <a href="/notes/stretch-hessian-singularities">Singularities in Stretch-Based Hessians</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The paper also proves that the resulting Hessian, like the stress, does not depend on which of the infinitely many
    valid <Tex tex="U, V" /> pairs the SVD returned, for two and for three equal stretches. Without that, the tangent
    stiffness could jump between Newton iterations for no physical reason.
  </p>
  <p>
    The twist quotient's denominator <Tex tex={String.raw`\lambda_i+\lambda_j`} /> vanishes only for inverted
    configurations. Whether that singularity is removable depends on the energy; for ARAP it is not, because the rotation of
    the polar decomposition really is undefined there.
  </p>

  <h2 id="rest">At rest, the spectrum is the Lamé parameters</h2>
  <p>
    Put the two papers together. At the rest shape all <Tex tex={String.raw`\psi_i`} /> vanish, so every twist eigenvalue is
    0 (rotating an undeformed element costs nothing). By symmetry the 3×3 block has only <Tex tex={String.raw`\psi_{11}`} />
    on its diagonal and <Tex tex={String.raw`\psi_{12}`} /> off it, and the flip limit is
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\psi_{11}-\psi_{12}`} />. With the Lamé parameters of an arbitrary material defined as
    <Tex tex={String.raw`\lambda_{\text{Lamé}}=\psi_{12}`} />, <Tex tex={String.raw`\mu_{\text{Lamé}}=\tfrac12(\psi_{11}-\psi_{12})`} />
    (SCA 2026, Eqs. 5–6), the nine eigenvalues at rest are
  </p>
  <Tex block tex={String.raw`\underbrace{3\lambda_{\text{Lamé}}+2\mu_{\text{Lamé}}}_{\text{once: volume change}},\qquad
    \underbrace{2\mu_{\text{Lamé}}}_{\text{five times: shears}},\qquad
    \underbrace{0}_{\text{three times: rotations}},`} />
  <p>
    for <em>any</em> isotropic material, whatever its formula. The first is three times the bulk modulus; the five shear
    modes all see the same shear modulus, which is what isotropy means at the linear level. I verified this with
    finite-difference Hessians of Neo-Hookean, St. Venant–Kirchhoff and Stable Neo-Hookean at the identity and at a random
    rotation. It is also a quick unit test for any new material implementation.
  </p>

  <h2 id="demo">Try it</h2>
  <p>
    Choose two stretches and a rotation. The bars show the four eigenvalues of the 2D Hessian from the formulas; the dots
    show the eigenvalues of a Hessian computed by finite differences on <Tex tex={String.raw`\Psi(F)`} />, with no SVD
    formulas involved. The four small squares wobble along each eigen-mode. Set both stretches to 1: in 2D the bars read
    <Tex tex={String.raw`2(\lambda+\mu)`} />, <Tex tex={String.raw`2\mu`} />, <Tex tex={String.raw`2\mu`} /> and 0, the 2D
    version of the rest spectrum above, and the flip uses its limit. Then try ARAP with both stretches below 1: the twist
    eigenvalue <Tex tex={String.raw`2\mu\,(1-2/(\lambda_1+\lambda_2))`} /> goes negative.
  </p>
  <figure class="note-fig hes-demo">
    <canvas id="hes-canvas" width="880" height="420" aria-label="Interactive demo: Hessian eigenvalue bars with finite-difference checks, and four animated eigen-mode squares."></canvas>
    <div class="hes-controls">
      <label><Tex tex={String.raw`\lambda_1`} /> <input type="range" id="hes-s1" min="0.5" max="2" step="0.01" value="1.3" /> <span id="hes-s1v"></span></label>
      <label><Tex tex={String.raw`\lambda_2`} /> <input type="range" id="hes-s2" min="-0.4" max="2" step="0.01" value="0.8" /> <span id="hes-s2v"></span></label>
      <label>rotation <input type="range" id="hes-rot" min="-3.14" max="3.14" step="0.01" value="0.5" /></label>
      <label>material <select id="hes-sel">
        <option value="arap">ARAP (μ = 1)</option>
        <option value="neohookean">Neo-Hookean (μ = 1, λ = 2)</option>
      </select></label>
    </div>
    <div class="hes-read" id="hes-read"></div>
    <figcaption>Left: eigenvalues from the closed-form formulas (bars) and from a finite-difference Hessian (dots); bars
      below the axis are the directions a projected Newton method clamps. Right: each eigen-mode, animated.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2 id="practice">Projection to positive definiteness</h2>
  <p>
    Newton's method needs a positive semi-definite tangent, and many energies are not convex everywhere (ARAP under
    compression, almost anything near inversion). With the block-diagonal form, projection is cheap and exact: eigendecompose
    the 3×3 block and clamp its eigenvalues, clamp the six scalars, and rebuild with <Tex tex="B" />. In the paper this
    projection, together with the singularity handling, is what turned stretch-based simulation from unstable (Figure 7, top)
    into robust enough to sit inside a material-fitting optimizer (Figure 7, bottom). Making the per-element stiffness
    definite in the SVD frame goes back at least to Teran et al. (SCA 2005), building on the signed SVD of Irving et al. (SCA 2004).
  </p>

  <h2 id="checklist">A short checklist</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Use the signed SVD (proper rotations, one negative stretch when <Tex tex={String.raw`\det F<0`} />), or the formulas
      silently describe a different deformation.</li>
    <li>Guard the flip quotient with its limit <Tex tex={String.raw`\psi_{ii}-\psi_{ij}`} /> when two stretches are within about
      <Tex tex={String.raw`10^{-4}`} />.</li>
    <li>Test at rest: the nine eigenvalues must be <Tex tex={String.raw`3\lambda_{\text{Lamé}}+2\mu_{\text{Lamé}}`} />,
      <Tex tex={String.raw`2\mu_{\text{Lamé}}`} /> (five times) and 0 (three times).</li>
    <li>Check <Tex tex="P" /> against finite differences of <Tex tex={String.raw`\Psi`} />, then each closed-form eigenpair
      against a finite-difference Hessian applied to its mode.</li>
    <li>Project by clamping the nine eigenvalues, not by adding a multiple of the identity: it keeps the stiffness exact in
      the directions that were already fine.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="refs">References</h2>
  <ul class="refs">
    <li>H. Chen, D. Zhao and J. Barbič. Capturing Animation-Ready Isotropic Materials Using Systematic Poking. <em>ACM Trans. on Graphics</em> (SIGGRAPH Asia 2023) 42(6), 2023. Section 5, Figures 6–7, Appendices C and D.</li>
    <li>H. Chen and J. Barbič. Weak Nonlinearity and Linear Equalization for Isotropic Hyperelastic Materials. <em>Computer Graphics Forum</em> 45(8) (Proc. SCA), 2026. Theorem 1, Eqs. 5–6.</li>
    <li>B. Smith, F. de Goes and T. Kim. Analytic Eigensystems for Isotropic Distortion Energies. <em>ACM Trans. on Graphics</em> 38(1), 2019.</li>
    <li>J. M. Ball. Differentiability Properties of Symmetric and Isotropic Functions. <em>Duke Mathematical Journal</em> 51(3), 699–728, 1984.</li>
    <li>J. Teran, E. Sifakis, G. Irving and R. Fedkiw. Robust Quasistatic Finite Elements and Flesh Simulation. <em>Symp. on Computer Animation</em>, 181–190, 2005.</li>
    <li>G. Irving, J. Teran and R. Fedkiw. Invertible Finite Elements for Robust Simulation of Large Deformation. <em>Symp. on Computer Animation</em>, 131–140, 2004.</li>
    <li>H. Xu, F. Sin, Y. Zhu and J. Barbič. Nonlinear Material Design Using Principal Stretches. <em>ACM Trans. on Graphics</em> (SIGGRAPH 2015) 34(4), 2015.</li>
  </ul>


<style>
  .hes-demo canvas { width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 6px; }
  .hes-controls { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: .5em 1.2em; align-items: center; margin-top: .6em; }
  .hes-controls input[type=range] { vertical-align: middle; width: 8em; }
  .hes-controls select { font: inherit; background: var(--bg); color: var(--ink); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 4px; padding: .15em .3em; }
  .hes-read { margin-top: .6em; font-size: .9em; overflow-x: auto; }
  .hes-read table { border-collapse: collapse; }
  .hes-read td, .hes-read th { padding: .15em 1.6em .15em 0; text-align: left; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  .refs { font-size: .9em; }
  .refs li { margin: .35em 0; }
</style>

<script>
  import { MATS, rot, mul, analyticEigen, fdHessian, symEig } from '../scripts/isotropic-gradient-hessian.js';
  const cv = document.getElementById('hes-canvas') as HTMLCanvasElement;
  const ctx = cv.getContext('2d')!;
  const W = cv.width, H = cv.height;
  const $ = (id: string) => document.getElementById(id) as HTMLInputElement;
  const css = (v: string) => getComputedStyle(document.documentElement).getPropertyValue(v).trim() || '#000';
  const SQUARE = [[-1, -1], [1, -1], [1, 1], [-1, 1]];
  let state: any = null, visible = true, t0 = performance.now();

  function compute() {
    const m = (MATS as any)[$('hes-sel').value];
    if ($('hes-sel').value === 'neohookean') { $('hes-s2').min = '0.3'; if (+$('hes-s2').value < 0.3) $('hes-s2').value = '0.3'; }
    else $('hes-s2').min = '-0.4';
    const s1 = +$('hes-s1').value, s2 = +$('hes-s2').value, th = +$('hes-rot').value;
    $('hes-s1v').textContent = s1.toFixed(2); $('hes-s2v').textContent = s2.toFixed(2);
    const F = mul(rot(th), [s1, 0, 0, s2]);
    const an = analyticEigen(m, F);
    const num = symEig(fdHessian(m, F), 4).values;
    // match each analytic eigenvalue to the closest unused numeric one
    const used = new Set<number>();
    const fd = an.map((a) => { let best = -1; num.forEach((x, i) => { if (!used.has(i) && (best < 0 || Math.abs(x - a.value) < Math.abs(num[best] - a.value))) best = i; }); used.add(best); return num[best]; });
    state = { F, an, fd };
    const worst = Math.max(...an.map((a, i) => Math.abs(a.value - fd[i])));
    (document.getElementById('hes-read') as HTMLElement).innerHTML =
      `<table><tr><th>mode</th>${an.map((a) => `<th>${a.name}</th>`).join('')}</tr>` +
      `<tr><td>formula</td>${an.map((a) => `<td>${a.value.toFixed(4)}</td>`).join('')}</tr>` +
      `<tr><td>finite differences</td>${fd.map((x) => `<td>${x.toFixed(4)}</td>`).join('')}</tr></table>` +
      `<span class="muted">Largest difference ${worst.toExponential(1)} (finite-difference error).` +
      `${Math.abs(s1 - s2) < 1e-6 ? ' λ₁ = λ₂: flip uses the limit ψ₁₁ − ψ₁₂.' : ''}</span>`;
  }

  function draw(now: number) {
    if (!state) return;
    const ink = css('--ink'), muted = css('--muted'), link = css('--link'), line = css('--line'), bg = css('--bg');
    ctx.fillStyle = bg; ctx.fillRect(0, 0, W, H);
    // bars
    const bx = 30, bw = 380, by0 = 40, bh = H - 90;
    const vals = state.an.map((a: any) => a.value).concat(state.fd);
    const vmax = Math.max(1, ...vals.map(Math.abs)) * 1.15;
    const zeroY = by0 + bh / 2, sy = (v: number) => zeroY - (v / vmax) * (bh / 2);
    ctx.strokeStyle = line; ctx.lineWidth = 1; ctx.beginPath(); ctx.moveTo(bx, zeroY); ctx.lineTo(bx + bw, zeroY); ctx.stroke();
    ctx.font = '13px system-ui, sans-serif'; ctx.textAlign = 'center';
    state.an.forEach((a: any, i: number) => {
      const x = bx + 20 + i * (bw - 40) / 4, w = (bw - 40) / 4 - 24;
      ctx.fillStyle = a.value < 0 ? link : muted; ctx.globalAlpha = a.value < 0 ? 0.9 : 0.55;
      const y = sy(a.value); ctx.fillRect(x, Math.min(y, zeroY), w, Math.abs(zeroY - y)); ctx.globalAlpha = 1;
      ctx.fillStyle = ink; ctx.beginPath(); ctx.arc(x + w / 2, sy(state.fd[i]), 4.5, 0, 7); ctx.fill();
      ctx.fillText(a.name, x + w / 2, by0 + bh + 22); ctx.fillText(a.value.toFixed(2), x + w / 2, a.value >= 0 ? y - 8 : y + 18);
    });
    ctx.textAlign = 'left'; ctx.fillStyle = muted; ctx.fillText('Hessian eigenvalues', bx, 22);
    // modes
    const t = (now - t0) / 1000, amp = 0.28 * Math.sin(t * 2.4);
    const px0 = 450, pw = (W - px0 - 10) / 2, ph = (H - 20) / 2, sc = Math.min(pw, ph) / 4.6;
    state.an.forEach((a: any, i: number) => {
      const cx = px0 + pw * (i % 2) + pw / 2, cy = 10 + ph * Math.floor(i / 2) + ph / 2 + 6;
      const G = state.F.map((x: number, k: number) => x + amp * a.mode[k]);
      const P = (M: number[]) => SQUARE.map((p) => [cx + sc * (M[0] * p[0] + M[1] * p[1]), cy - sc * (M[2] * p[0] + M[3] * p[1])]);
      const path = (pts: number[][]) => { ctx.beginPath(); pts.forEach((p, j) => (j ? ctx.lineTo(p[0], p[1]) : ctx.moveTo(p[0], p[1]))); ctx.closePath(); };
      ctx.setLineDash([4, 4]); ctx.strokeStyle = muted; ctx.lineWidth = 1; path(P(state.F)); ctx.stroke(); ctx.setLineDash([]);
      ctx.strokeStyle = a.value < 0 ? link : ink; ctx.lineWidth = 2; path(P(G)); ctx.stroke();
      ctx.fillStyle = ink; ctx.textAlign = 'center'; ctx.fillText(`${a.name}  ${a.value.toFixed(2)}`, cx, cy - ph / 2 + 10);
    });
    ctx.textAlign = 'left';
  }

  function loop(now: number) { if (visible) draw(now); requestAnimationFrame(loop); }
  new IntersectionObserver((es) => { visible = es[0].isIntersecting; }).observe(cv);
  ['hes-s1', 'hes-s2', 'hes-rot', 'hes-sel'].forEach((id) => $(id).addEventListener('input', compute));
  compute(); requestAnimationFrame(loop);
</script>
